abstract: >-
  CarbonCreditBuy is an open-source, self-hostable geospatial platform that
  helps farmers, landowners, advisors and researchers estimate how much carbon
  the trees on one piece of land may remove, and what evidence is still
  missing before a recognised carbon or environmental programme could issue
  any credit. Users mark land on a map (boundary, freehand pen, rectangle,
  circle, tree line, pin with area, typed coordinates, or imported
  GeoJSON/KML/CSV/LAS/LAZ files). Deterministic engines combine
  Earth-observation layers (ESA WorldCover land cover, Copernicus Sentinel-2
  NDVI/NDMI) with configuration-driven parameters, each carrying a source and
  a verification status, to compute IPCC 2006 Tier 1 gain-loss estimates
  (Tier 2 with measured diameter using Chave et al. 2014) as low-central-high
  ranges with a confidence grade and provenance. A policy engine screens
  programmes and tree-age rules for India, the United States and the European
  Union. A team of nine deterministic agents explains results under six
  guardrails; an optional OpenAI-compatible language model may only rephrase
  redacted text. Explanations use one-at-a-time sensitivity re-runs of the
  engine rather than a learned model. Every output is a scenario estimate,
  not a carbon credit (certified: false). Demo mode uses synthetic data;
  agreement with field measurements has not yet been evaluated.
keywords:
